WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.160
Let's say you're trying to lose 20 pounds,
or boost your immunity,

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:07.680
or increase your ability to fight COVID,
or even cancer.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.840
Well, the amazing thing is,
with the right diet,

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.040
you're well on your way to
achieving these vital health goals.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.480
Welcome to the Nutrition Facts Podcast.
I'm your host, Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.200
Today, we have the first in a four-
part series on bariatric surgery,

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.840
the most common of which are
stomach stapling and gastric bypass,

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:34.960
and both of which involve
change to one’s digestive anatomy.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.000
So how safe are
these procedures,

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.080
and how well do they
work for weight loss?

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:41.600
Let’s find out.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:46.240
The treatment of obesity has
long been stained by the snake oil

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:49.280
swindling of profiteers,
hustlers, and quacks.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.440
Even the modern field
of bariatric medicine

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.680
(derived from the Greek word
baros meaning weight)

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:58.160
is pervaded by an “insidious
image of sleaze.”

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.480
Beguiled by advertising for
fairy tale magic bullets of rapid,

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:05.520
effortless weight loss, people
blame themselves for failing

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:09.120
to manifest the miracle or imagine
themselves metabolically broken.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:13.200
On the other end of the spectrum
are overly pessimistic practitioners

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.640
of the opinion that people
who are fat are born fat,

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:18.080
and nothing can be done about it.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.200
The truth lies somewhere in between.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.320
The difficulty of curing
obesity has been compared

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:25.840
to learning a foreign language.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.640
It’s an achievement
virtually anyone can attain

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:31.360
with a sufficient
investment of energies,

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:34.240
but it always takes
considerable time and effort.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:36.640
And of those who do stick with it,

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:39.120
most will regain much of the weight lost.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:43.920
To me this speaks to the difficulty,
rather than the futility.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.360
It may take smokers an
average of 30 quit attempts

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:48.400
to finally kick the habit.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:52.560
Like quitting smoking, it’s just
something that has to be done.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.440
As the Chair of the Association
for the Study of Obesity put it,

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:59.120
it doesn’t take “willpower” to
do essential tasks like getting up

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:02.720
at night to feed a baby; it’s just
something that has to be done.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:06.880
Our collective response doesn’t
seem to match the rhetoric or reality.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:11.040
If obesity is such a “national crisis”
“reaching alarming proportions,”

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:13.280
dubbed by the post-9/11
Surgeon General as

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:15.440
“every bit as devastating as terrorism,”

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:19.200
why has our reaction been so tepid?

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.200
For example, governments meekly
suggest the food industry take

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.440
“voluntary initiatives to
restrict the marketing of

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.200
less healthy food options to children.”

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.400
Have we just given up
and ceded control?

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:34.800
Our timid response to the
obesity epidemic is encapsulated

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.320
by a national initiative promulgated
by a Joint Task Force

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.560
of the American Society for Nutrition,
Institute of Food Technologists,

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:44.480
and International Food
Information Council:

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.880
the “small-changes approach.”

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:50.240
Since small changes are “more feasible,”

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:53.520
suggestions include “using
mustard instead of mayonnaise”

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:56.560
and “eating 1 rather than
2 doughnuts in the morning.”

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:00.720
Seems a bit like bringing
a butter knife to a gunfight,

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:02.320
but with only one croissant.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:05.760
Proponents of the small-
changes approach lament that

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:09.600
unlike other addictions—e.g. alcohol,
cocaine, gambling, or tobacco—

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.040
we can’t counsel our obese patients
to give up the addictive element

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.160
completely, as “no one
can give up eating.”

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:20.000
But just because we have to eat,
doesn’t mean we have to eat junk,

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:22.600
like just because we have
to breathe, doesn’t mean

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:25.440
it has to be through the
end of a cigarette.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:29.360
What about bringing a scalpel
to the gunfight instead?

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.760
The use of bariatric surgery has
exploded from about 40,000 procedures

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:37.120
noted in the first international
survey in 1998 to now

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:40.640
hundreds of thousands performed
every year in the United States alone.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:43.920
The first technique developed,
the intestinal bypass,

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:47.120
involved carving out
about 19 feet of intestines.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:50.640
More than 30,000 intestinal bypass
operations were performed

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:55.440
before “catastrophic,” “disastrous
outcomes” were recognized,

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:58.000
including protein deficiency-
induced liver disease,

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.280
which often progressed to
liver failure and death.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.480
This inauspicious start
is remembered as

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:08.160
“one of the dark blots in
the history of surgery.”

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:13.040
Today, death rates after bariatric
surgery are considered “very low,”

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:18.080
occurring on average in perhaps
1 in 300 to 1 in 500 patients.

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:21.200
The most common procedure is
stomach stapling, also known as

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:25.520
sleeve gastrectomy, in which most
of the stomach is permanently removed.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:28.720
Only a narrow tube of stomach
is left, so as to restrict

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:30.880
how much food people can
eat at any one time.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.720
It’s ironic that many patients
choose bariatric surgery

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:37.680
convinced that “diets
don’t work” for them,

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:42.080
when in reality that’s all the
surgery may be—an enforced diet.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:46.080
Bariatric surgery can be thought
of as a form of internal jaw wiring.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:50.320
Gastric bypass, known as
Roux-en-Y gastric bypass,

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:52.640
is the second most common
bariatric surgery.

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:55.840
It combines restriction—stapling
the stomach into a pouch

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:58.880
smaller than a golf ball—
with malabsorption

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:00.960
by rearranging your anatomy

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:03.520
to bypass the first part
of your small intestine.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:06.720
It appears to be more effective than
just cutting out most of the stomach.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.920
It results in a loss of
about 63% of excess weight

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:12.640
compared to 53% with a gastric sleeve.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:16.560
But gastric bypass carries a
greater risk of serious complications.

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:20.400
Many are surprised to learn
that new surgical procedures

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:23.920
don’t require premarket
testing or FDA approval

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:27.840
and are largely exempt from
rigorous regulatory scrutiny.

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:32.800
In our next story we look
at how the extent of risk

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:38.514
from bariatric weight-loss surgery
may depend on the skill of the surgeon.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:43.200
After sleeve gastrectomy and
Roux-en-Y gastric bypass,

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:47.680
the third most common
bariatric procedure is a revision

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.240
to fix a previous bariatric procedure.

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:53.680
Up to 25% of bariatric patients
have to go back into the

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:57.440
operating room for problems caused
by their first bariatric surgery.

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:01.040
And re-operations are riskier,
carrying around 10 times

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:04.720
the mortality rate and offer
no guarantee of success.

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:09.120
Complications include leaks,
fistulas, ulcers, strictures,

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:12.000
erosions, obstructions,
and severe acid reflux.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:16.720
The extent of risk may depend
on the skill of the surgeon.

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:19.200
In a study published in the
New England Journal of Medicine,

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:23.200
bariatric surgeons voluntarily
submitted videos of themselves

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:26.640
performing surgery to a panel
of their peers for evaluation.

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:30.800
Technical proficiency varied
widely and was related to the rates

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:35.120
of complications, hospital readmissions,
re-operations, and death.

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:38.880
Patients operated on by the
less competent surgeons suffered

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:42.240
nearly three times the complications
and five times the risk of death.

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:47.440
As with athletes and musicians,
some surgeons may simply be more

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:50.800
talented than others, but
practice may help make perfect.

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:55.040
Gastric bypass is such a complicated
procedure that the learning curve

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:59.360
may require 500 cases for a
surgeon to master the procedure.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:02.880
Complications risk plateaus
after about 500 cases,

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:05.280
with the lowest risk
found among surgeons

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:07.440
who’ve performed more
than 600 bypasses.

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:12.480
The risk of not making it out alive
may be double under the knife

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:17.120
of those who’ve done less than
75 compared to more than 450.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:20.160
So, if you do choose to undergo
the operation, I’d recommend

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:23.360
asking your surgeon how
many procedures they’ve done,

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:27.280
as well as choosing an accredited
bariatric “Center of Excellence,”

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:30.160
where surgical mortality appears
to be two to three times lower

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:32.000
than non-accredited institutions.

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:35.360
It’s not always the
surgeon’s fault, though.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:39.440
In a report entitled “The Dangers
of Broccoli,” a surgeon described

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:41.840
a case in which a woman went
to an all-you-can-eat buffet

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:44.240
three months after a
gastric bypass operation.

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:46.560
She chose really healthy
foods (good for her!).

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:50.240
She just evidently forgot to chew.

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:54.560
Her staples blew, and she ended
up in the ER and then the OR.

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:57.040
They opened her up, and found
“full chunks of broccoli,

00:07:57.040 --> 00:07:59.520
whole lima beans, and other
green leafy vegetables”

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:01.200
inside her abdominal cavity.

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:06.000
A cautionary tale to be sure, but
perhaps less about chewing food better

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:09.840
after surgery than about chewing
better foods before surgery,

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:14.080
so you can keep all your internal
organs intact in the first place.

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:16.880
Even if the surgical
procedure goes perfectly,

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:19.600
lifelong nutritional replacement
and monitoring are required

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:22.080
to avoid vitamin and mineral deficits.

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:25.760
This includes more than a little
anemia, osteoporosis, or hair loss,

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:29.280
but full-blown cases of life-
threatening deficiencies,

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:32.640
such as beriberi, pellagra,
kwashiorkor, and nerve damage

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:34.720
that can manifest as vision loss years

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:38.160
or even decades after surgery in
the case of copper deficiency.

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:41.360
Tragically, in cases
of severe deficiency

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:43.280
of a B vitamin called thiamine,

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:46.880
nearly one in three patients progressed
to permanent brain damage

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:48.880
before the condition was caught.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:53.360
The malabsorption of
nutrients is on purpose

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:55.120
for procedures like gastric bypass.

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:58.640
By cutting out segments of the
intestines you can successfully

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:01.760
impair the absorption of
calories, but at the expense

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:04.320
of impairing the absorption
of necessary nutrition.

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:07.440
Even people who just undergo
restrictive procedures like

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:10.320
stomach stapling can be at
risk for life-threatening

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:14.960
nutrient deficiencies
because of persistent vomiting.

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:18.480
Indeed, vomiting is reported
by up to 60% of patients

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:22.640
after bariatric surgery due to
“inappropriate” eating behaviors

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:24.560
(in other words, trying to eat normally).

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:28.480
The vomiting helps with weight
loss similar to the way

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:31.920
a drug for alcoholics called
Antabuse can be used to make them

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:36.320
so violently ill after a drink that they
eventually can learn their lesson.

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:39.360
“Dumping syndrome”
can work the same way.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:42.400
A large percentage of gastric
bypass patients can suffer

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:45.920
from abdominal pain, diarrhea,
nausea, bloating, fatigue,

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:48.400
or palpitations after
eating calorie-rich foods

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:52.400
as they bypass your stomach and
dump straight into your intestines.

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:56.480
As surgeons describe it,
this is a feature, not a bug:

00:09:56.480 --> 00:09:59.920
“Dumping syndrome is an
expected and desired part of

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:03.200
the behavior modiﬁcation caused
by gastric bypass surgery;

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:07.592
it can deter patients from
consuming energy-dense food.”

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:12.240
